              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesHistoricalOrigins"} Historical origins
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further"} Further
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em topological space
            aims to axiomatize the idea of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            as a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            that hang together (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"}> cohere
            ”) in a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
            way. Roughly speaking, a
            %em topology
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            “of points” prescribes which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            are to be considered “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhoods"}> neighborhoods
            ” of the points they contain. Various conditions or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            must be satisfied in order for such neighborhood systems to form a topology, but one of the most important is that for any two neighborhoods of a point, their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            must also be a neighborhood of that point.
          %p
            Many notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            have
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+topological+spaces"} underlying topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+spaces"} probability spaces
          %p
            The concept of a topology, gradually refined over the latter half of the 19th century and the first two decades of the 20th, was developed to capture what it means abstractly for a mapping between sets of points to be “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"}> continuous
            ”. Intuitively, the idea of
            = succeed "," do
              %em bending
            %em twisting
            or
            %em crumpling
            a continuous body applies to continuous mappings, because they preserve neighborhood relations (in a suitable sense), but
            = succeed "," do
              %em tearing
          %p
            For example, the surface of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            or doughnut is topologically equivalent to the surface of a mug: the surface of the mug can be deformed
            %em continuously
            into the surface of a torus. Abstractly speaking: the continuous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            among the collections of points of the two surfaces is the same. Similarly, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square"} square
            are considered equivalent from the standpoint of their topologies.
          %p
            Some one-dimensional shapes with different topologies: the Mercedes-Benz symbol, a line, a circle, a complete graph with 5 nodes, the skeleton of a cube, and an asterisk (or, if you’ll permit the one-dimensional approximation, a starfish). On the other hand, a circle has the same topology as a line segment with a wormhole at its finish which teleports you to its start; or more prosaically: The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %em endpoints identified
          %p
            There is a generalization of the notion of topological spaces to that of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhoods"} neighbourhoods
            are explicitly or even necessarily supported by points. For this reason, the theory of locales is sometimes called “pointless topology”. In this form, the definition turns out to be quite fundamental and can be naturally motivated from just pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            – as the formal dual of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            – as well as, and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dually
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            in its variant as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            – by the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-toposes"} (0,1)-toposes
          %p
            Topological spaces are the objects studied in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            Topological spaces equipped with extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} properties
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            form the fundament of much of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            into it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            on which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is based. And so on.
          %h2#Definitions Definitions
          %p We present first the
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#StandardDefinition"} standard definition
          %p and then a list of different
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AlternateDefinitions"}> equivalent definitions
              \.
          %p Finally we mention genuine
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Variants"}> variants of the notion
              \.
          %h3#StandardDefinition Standard definition
          %p
          #TheStandardDefinition.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong topological space
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              equipped with a set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  finite
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
              %li
                %p
                  arbitrary
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            %p
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The word ‘topology’ sometimes means the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} study of topological spaces
              but here it means the collection of open sets in a topological space. In particular, if someone says ‘Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be a topology on
              = succeed "\u2019," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be equipped with the structure of a topological space, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be the collection of open sets in this space’.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              itself is the intersection of zero subsets, it is open, and since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
              is the union of zero subsets, it is also open. Moreover, every open subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              contains the empty set and is contained in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\emptyset \subset U \subset X
                    \\,,
            %p
              so that the topology of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is determined by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+open+subsets"} poset of open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom"} bottom
              element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot = \emptyset
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top"} top
              element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top = X
            %p
              Since by definition the elements in this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              are closed under finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
              (intersection) and arbitrary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
              (unions), this poset of open subsets defining a topology is a
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+of+opens"} frame of opens
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              between topological spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is a
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            %p
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
              of the underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
              of every open set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is an open set of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Topological spaces with continuous maps between them form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              The definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is such that it induces a homomorphism of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames+of+opens"} frames of opens
              the other way around
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ←
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Op(X) \leftarrow Op(Y) : f^{-1}
                    \\,.
            %p
              And this is not just a morphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              but even of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
          %h3#AlternateDefinitions Alternate equivalent definitions
          %p There are many equivalent ways to define a topological space. A non-exhaustive list follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
                of open sets (the standard definition, given above), called a
                %em topology
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                A set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with a co-frame of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+sets"} closed sets
                (the complements of the open sets), satisfying dual axioms: closure under finite unions and arbitrary intersections. This is sometimes called a co-topology on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                A pair
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi int
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, int)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int\colon P(X) \to P(X)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+functor"} left exact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interior"} interior
                operator”). In more nuts-and-bolts terms, this means for all subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B \Rightarrow int(A) \subseteq int(B), \;\;\; int(A) \subseteq A, \;\;\; int(A) \subseteq int(int(A)), \;\;\; int(A \cap B) = int(A) \cap int(B), \;\;\; int(X) = X.
              %p
                The open sets are exactly the fixed points of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi int
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} coclosure operator
            %li
              %p
                A pair
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, cl)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cl
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rex+functor"} right exact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+closure"} Moore closure
                operator satisfying axioms dual to those of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi int
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi cl
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cl
                %em Kuratowski closure operator
                (compare Kuratowski’s closure-complement problem at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed subspace
            %li
              %p
                A set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                together with, for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_x
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                closed under finite intersections and also upward-closed (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in N_x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq V
                together imply
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⊆
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subseteq N_x
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in N_x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                a
                %em neighborhood
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
                for every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in N_x
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in N_x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in N_x
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subseteq U
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is a neighborhood of each point it contains. In this formulation, a subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
                is
                %em open
                if it is a neighborhood of every point it contains.
          %p
            The next two definitions of topological space are at a higher level of abstraction, but the underlying idea that connects them with the neighborhood system formulation is that we say a filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em converges
            to a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_x \subseteq F
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relational+beta-module"} relational beta-module
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+algebra"} lax algebra
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilters"} ultrafilters
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C2%29-category"} (1,2)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
                of sets and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relations"} binary relations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                together with a relation called “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"}> convergence
                ” between ultrafilters and points satisfying certain axioms. This exhibits it as a special sort of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudotopological+space"} pseudotopological space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+principle"} ultrafilter principle
                to be true.
            %li
              %p
                A set with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+relation"} convergence relation
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
                (not just ultrafilters) and points, or even between transfinite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
                and points, satisfying appropriate axioms.
          %p The following are not definitions, but they provide alternative ways to present a topological space.
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+base"} (topological) base
              on a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              of subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              whose union is all of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B, C \in \mathcal{B}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in B \cap C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in \mathcal{B}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \subseteq B \cap C
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in D
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            is a base on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            is a topology on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              A set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with any collection of subsets whatsoever, to be thought of as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbase
              for a topology.
          %p
            From the fact that the intersection of any collection of topologies is also a topology, there is a smallest topology that contains a given subbase
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %em topology generated by
            the subbase.
          %h3#Variants Variations
          %p
            Historically, the notion of topological space (see the historical references given
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "topology#ReferencesHistoricalOrigins"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhoods"} neighbourhoods
            was first developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Hausdorff"} Felix Hausdorff
            in 1914 in his seminal text on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %em Fundamentals of Set Theory
            (
            %em>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grundz%C3%BCge+der+Mengenlehre"} Grundzüge der Mengenlehre
            ). Hausdorff’s definition originally contained the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            (now known as the definition of
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kazimierz+Kuratowski"} Kazimierz Kuratowski
            in 1922, who defined general topological spaces in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operators"} closure operators
            that preserve finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+set"} open set
            formulation was widely popularized by Bourbaki in their 1940 treatise (without identifying a single author behind this notion).
          %p
            However, in more modern treatments that emphasize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            methods, particularly to address needs of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of all topological spaces, but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+categories+of+topological+spaces"} convenient categories of topological spaces
            that are better behaved, especially with regard to function spaces and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+topological+spaces"} nice topological spaces
            (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+topological+spaces"} sequential topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice-
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+topological+spaces"} convenient category of topological spaces
            (such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+spaces"} compactly generated spaces
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoids
            (such as
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %p
            On the other hand, when doing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            or working in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            than topological spaces.
          %p
            Some applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            require more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+spaces"} convergence spaces
            or other generalisations.
          %p
            There are also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unified+topological+spaces"} unified topological spaces
            which contains notions of apartness and nearness in addition to the usual notion of neighbourhood in a topological space.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            be a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            instead of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T0-space"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            forces the type to be an h-set.
          %h3#DependentTypeTheory  In dependent type theory
          %p
            In
